REAL EXAMPLE:
When I open my mobile banking app to check my balance, my request travels down through all 7 OSI layers before hitting the internet.

Here is how each layer handles my request step-by-step:

1. Application: my bank app generates the request to view my account balance using HTTPS.
2. Presentation: The app uses TLS to encrypt my login credentials and balance request so hackers can't read it.
3. Session: A secure communication channel is opened and maintained between my phone and the bank's server.
4. Transport: TCP breaks my encrypted request into smaller chunks (segments) and ensures none of them get lost along the way.
5. Network: IP wraps those segments into packets, attaches the bank's destination IP address, and finds the best route across the internet.
6. Data Link: my local Wi-Fi or cellular router adds MAC addresses to frame the data for local physical transmission.
7. Physical: my phone's antenna converts the data into raw electrical or radio wave bits (0s and 1s) and transmits them through the air.


When the bank's back-end servers receive my balance request, the data travels upward through the OSI layers in reverse. 
Here is how the server processes my incoming request:

1. Physical (Layer 7 in reverse / Layer 1): The bank’s fiber-optic cables or network interface card (NIC) pick up the incoming electrical or light signals from the internet.
2. Data Link (Layer 2): The server's network card strips the MAC framing and verifies that the incoming packet is explicitly meant for this server.
3. Network (Layer 3): The server checks the IP packet header to confirm it arrived at the correct destination address within the bank's data center.
4. Transport (Layer 4): TCP reassembles the incoming data chunks into a complete stream and checks that nothing was corrupted during transit.
5. Session (Layer 5): The server matches the incoming request to my active login session using tokens or session identifiers.
6. Presentation (Layer 6): TLS decrypts the encrypted payload, translating the secure code back into readable web traffic.
7. Application (Layer 7): The bank's web server application processes the HTTPS request, securely queries the core database for my balance, 
                          and prepares the response to send back down to my phone.



ENCAPSULATION:
==============
A process of adding headers and trailers to raw data for sending.

Encapsulation is like packing a nested shipping box as my bank balance request moves down the OSI stack:

 Layers 7–5 (App/Pres/Session): my "check balance" request is created, encrypted, and tagged with my session token.
 Layer 4 (Transport): Wrapped in a TCP header for reliable end-to-end delivery.
 Layer 3 (Network): Wrapped in an IP header for global internet routing.
 Layer 2 (Data Link): Wrapped in a MAC header for local device-to-router delivery.
 Layer 1 (Physical): Converted into raw bits (electrical or radio signals) for transmission.

The bank's server then unwraps each layer in reverse (decapsulation) to process my request.





===================  PORT and PROTOCOL  =================================

PORT:
-----
A port is a 16-bit number used to identify specific applications and services.
Port numbers are from 0 to 65535. The first 1024 ports are reserved for use by certain privileged services:




• FTP (File Transfer Protocol) (Ports 20/21)
    • Definition: A reliable way to upload and download files between a user and a server. Requires a login.
    • Example: Uploading website files from a laptop to a web hosting company.

• TFTP (Trivial File Transfer Protocol) (Port 69)
    • Definition: A fast, simple way to send files without a password. Uses UDP.
    • Example: Sending a basic update file to a network router.

• SSH (Secure Shell) (Port 22)
    • Definition: A highly secure, encrypted connection to control a remote computer.
    • Example: Logging into a distant cloud server to type system commands safely.

• TELNET (Telecommunication Network) (Port 23)
    • Definition: An old, unencrypted connection to control a remote computer. Sends passwords in plain text.
    • Example: Managing a legacy switch inside an isolated lab network.

• HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) (Port 80)
    • Definition: The basic, unencrypted rule for loading standard web pages.
    • Example: Reading a public, unprotected news blog where security does not matter.

• HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure) (Port 443)
    • Definition: The modern, encrypted version of HTTP that keeps web browsing safe.
    • Example: Typing a credit card number into an online store like Amazon.

• SMTP / SMTPS (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol / Simple Mail Transfer Protocol Secure) (Ports 25, 587, 465)
    • Definition: The network protocol used for sending emails.
    • Example: Clicking the "Send" button inside an app like Outlook or Gmail.

• POP3 / POP3S (Post Office Protocol version 3 / Post Office Protocol version 3 Secure) (Ports 110 / 995)
    • Definition: Downloads emails to one single device and deletes them from the server.
    • Example: Downloading emails to an offline laptop so they disappear from your phone.

• IMAP / IMAPS (Internet Message Access Protocol / Internet Message Access Protocol Secure) (Ports 143 / 993)
    • Definition: Keeps emails on the server so they stay synchronized across multiple devices.
    • Example: Reading an email on a phone, and seeing it marked as read on a laptop.

• DNS (Domain Name System) (Port 53)
    • Definition: Matches human-friendly website names with computer-friendly IP addresses.
    • Example: Translating google.com into the number 142.250.190.46.

• DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) (Ports 67 / 68)
    • Definition: Automatically gives a temporary IP address to a device when it connects to a network.
    • Example: Getting internet access instantly on your phone when joining a coffee shop's Wi-Fi.

• NetBIOS (Network Basic Input/Output System) (Ports 137, 138, 139)
    • Definition: A legacy system that helps older computers share files and printers on a local network.
    • Example: Finding and using a shared office printer using its computer name.

• SNMP (Simple Network Management Protocol) (Ports 161 / 162)
    • Definition: Used by IT managers to monitor the health and performance of network devices.
    • Example: Sending a warning alert to a dashboard when a server gets too hot.

• SSL / TLS (Secure Sockets Layer / Transport Layer Security) (Variable Ports / 443)
    • Definition: Security layers that encrypt data traffic for other apps. TLS is the modern standard.
    • Example: The invisible encryption that protects data inside a mobile banking app.

• PAP (Password Authentication Protocol)
    • Definition: An obsolete login method that sends usernames and passwords without encryption.
    • Example: An old dial-up modem sending a password over standard telephone wires.

• PPTP (Point-to-Point Tunneling Protocol)
    • Definition: An old, outdated protocol used to create simple virtual private networks (VPNs).
    • Example: An employee connecting to a corporate office network from home in the year 2000.




Core Network & Transport Concepts

• ARP (Address Resolution Protocol)
    • Definition: Asks a local network to match a known IP address to a hardware MAC address.
    • Example: A computer shouting: "Who has IP 192.168.1.50? Give me your physical hardware address."

• ICMP (Internet Control Message Protocol)
    • Definition: A network protocol that sends error messages and status updates between machines.
    • Example: A router telling your PC that a website you want to visit cannot be reached.

• TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)
    • Definition: A smart, reliable connection that guarantees all data arrives perfectly without errors.
    • Example: Downloading a software update where missing a single file piece would break it.

• UDP (User Datagram Protocol)
    • Definition: A fast, connectionless transmission that sends data quickly without checking for errors.
    • Example: Streaming a live football match or playing a fast-paced online video game.

• PAP (Password Authentication Protocol)
    • Definition: An obsolete login method that sends usernames and passwords without encryption.
    • Example: An old dial-up modem sending a password over standard telephone wires.

• PPTP (Point-to-Point Tunneling Protocol)
    • Definition: An old, outdated protocol used to create simple virtual private networks (VPNs).
    • Example: An employee connecting to a corporate office network from home in the year 2000.

• HDLC (High-Level Data Link Control)
    • Definition: A direct rule for organizing data frames sent over a single physical link.
    • Example: Sending continuous network data between two dedicated office routers using a private line.

• Ping (Packet Internet Groper)
    • Definition: A quick test tool that measures the response time between two network devices.
    • Example: Checking if your home internet is working by testing the speed to Google's servers.






